==English==
{{wikipedia}}
{{wikipedia|Gladius (cephalopod)}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|gladius|t=Roman short sword, gladius}}. {{doublet|en|glaive}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɡlæd.i.əs/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|+|gladii}}

# {{lb|en|historical}} A Roman sword roughly two feet long.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1882|chapter=[[s:Popular_Science_Monthly/Volume_21/May_1882/The_Genesis_of_the_Sword|The Genesis of the Sword]]|title=Popular Science Monthly|volume=21|page=81
|passage=Finally, the Romans made the '''gladius'''—sharp, of highly-tempered steel, and strongly piercing—the first real sword (Figs. 17, 18, 19), of which only five specimens are now known to exist.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Pat Southern|title=The Roman Army: A Social and Institutional History|page=212|passage=The '''gladius''' was effective either for cutting or for thrusting and was used by legionaries and auxiliaries.}}
# {{lb|en|zoology}} A [[pen]], a hard internal [[bodypart]] of certain [[cephalopod]]s, made of [[chitin]]-like material.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=31 October 2017|author=Mark Carnall|journal=The Guardian|url=https://www.theguardian.com/science/2017/oct/31/searching-for-the-old-ones-lovecraftian-giant-cephalopods-fossil-record
|passage=From the Cretaceous of North America fossilised '''gladii''' in the enigmatic genus ''Tusoteuthis'' have been estimated to give a mantle length (body size) of 1.8m, just less than that of the giant squid’s.}}

